mdp: 1
theme: mono
forms: [page, slides, flyer]
kicker: Spec v0.1
title: The MDP format
---

# The MDP format
{.lead} One plain-text source compiles into many polished forms; the design is locked in the renderer.

The source stays as clean and diffable as ordinary Markdown. The author writes content and light intent only; the renderer composes each form and applies the presentation mechanics. This is the syntax strawman — see [VISION.md](VISION.md) for positioning.

---

## Architecture in brief

The rendering method is deterministic composition over a semantic representation, not templates and not live AI.

```mdp:flow
Declarative source -> Tree of typed meaning -> Each artifact is a solver -> Pure, reproducible render
```

The substrate is extensible in two units, both with the design lock intact: blocks (new typed content rendered across every artifact) and artifacts (new output types as new solvers over the tree).

---

## Principles

1. **The spec is a prompt.** It is written so an LLM can read it and emit valid MDP with no examples.
2. **The author cannot style.** There is no color, size, or font in the source. Slop becomes structurally impossible.
3. **Wrong input still renders.** An unknown directive or malformed block degrades to readable text, never a blank screen.
4. **Git-native.** Line-oriented and diffable. Humans and agents edit it like code.
5. **Many forms, one source.** The renderer compiles the same source into every form.

---

## The file

- Extension `.mdp`, UTF-8.
- A superset of CommonMark. Any valid MDP file is also readable as plain Markdown.
- Optional YAML frontmatter, then a Markdown body.

The frontmatter carries the format version (`mdp: 1`, required), a named `theme`, the `forms` to compile, an optional `title` and `kicker`, the `lang` and `dir` for writing direction, and an optional `brand-logo` asset reference. None of these carry colors or sizes — the author picks a designed system, not individual styles.

:::callout note
`dir: rtl` (auto-inferred for he, ar, fa, ur, yi) flips the whole layout, because the renderer uses logical CSS properties throughout. The author never positions anything.
:::

---

## Body additions

Standard Markdown blocks plus a small, closed set of additions.

- **Section breaks.** A `---` on its own line: a divider on a page, a slide break in slides, a panel divider on a flyer, a numbered section in a report, a column break on a one-pager, a paragraph break in a memo or letter.
- **Line roles.** A block may open with a role tag: `{.lead}` a standfirst, `{.cite}` an attribution. An unknown role is ignored and the text renders plainly.
- **Fenced directives.** `mdp:stats` (label-and-value figures), `mdp:compare` (options as side-by-side cards), and `mdp:flow` (a left-to-right sequence of steps joined by `->`). An unknown kind falls back to a normal code block, so it is never lost.
- **Triple-colon containers.** `:::callout <variant>` lifts a short passage out of the flow; variants are note, tip, cost, recommendation, and warning.

There is deliberately no raw HTML, no inline CSS, and no scripting in the source. Those are the doors slop walks through.

---

## Forms and mechanics (v1)

A form is an output shape. v1 ships seven.

```mdp:compare
# page
note: A calm scrolling document — the reading form.
- Stats render as a table.
- Quotes become indented pull quotes.

# slides
note: A full-screen click-through deck — the presenting form.
- Stats become big figures.
- List items reveal one at a time.

# flyer
note: One composed surface, a poster or one-pager — the at-a-glance form.
- Stats render as a figure band.
- Compare and callout go full-width.

# report
note: A paginated long-form document — the structured reading form.
- A cover and an auto table of contents.
- Numbered sections throughout.

# onepager
note: A single dense sheet, print-first — the executive leave-behind.
- A header band over a packed two-column grid.
- A footer closes the sheet.

# memo
note: An internal business memo.
- A To / From / Date / Re header.
- A tight single column under it.

# letter
note: Formal correspondence.
- Letterhead, date, and salutation.
- A body and a sign-off.
```

The mechanics the renderer owns: stat figures, pull quotes, columns (adjacent short sections compose side by side), and reveals (list items appear one at a time in slides).

---

## House style and non-goals

The house style is identical across forms and the author cannot reach it: one sans and one serif, two weights, a fixed modular scale; one neutral ink ramp plus a single restrained accent; a single spacing scale; no gradients, no shadows, no decorative icons; minimal motion; WCAG AA contrast and full keyboard navigation.

Non-goals for v1: no arbitrary HTML or JavaScript, no inline styling, no per-form authoring, no animation authoring.

:::callout warning
A conforming tool validates the frontmatter and each directive body and returns line-level errors with a suggested fix, so an agent can correct its own output in one pass. (v0.1: contract defined, validator to follow.)
:::
